# Project Bonfire — machine-local settings (template)
#
# Copy this file to `.env` beside it and fill in the values for your machine:
#
#     copy .env.example .env      (Windows)
#     cp .env.example .env        (macOS / Linux)
#
# `.env` is gitignored; this template is tracked. Keep real paths, IP
# addresses and access codes out of it.
#
# Format: KEY=value, one per line. `#` starts a comment. No quotes and no
# escaping — the value is taken literally to the end of the line, so paths with
# spaces and backslashes are fine as they are. An empty value counts as unset.
#
# Who reads what:
#   * Bonfire's own tools read this file    (tools/env.py → load()).
#   * The Blender MCP server does NOT. It is started by the Claude desktop app
#     and only sees your user environment. After filling in BLENDER_PATH, run
#     tools/env.py → apply_command() and do what it prints, then restart the
#     desktop app.


# ── Required ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Absolute path to the Blender executable — the file itself, not its folder.
# The MCP server needs it for background (CLI) mode, which is what lets Blender
# be driven with no window open. Without it, it looks for `blender` on PATH and
# fails when it isn't there.
#
# To find it: in Blender, Scripting workspace → `bpy.app.binary_path`.
# Typically:
#   Windows   C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender <version>\blender.exe
#   macOS     /Applications/Blender.app/Contents/MacOS/Blender
#   Linux     /usr/bin/blender, or <where you unpacked it>/blender
#
# Use the same Blender you model in. If several versions are installed, naming
# the one you want here is what stops background mode picking another.
BLENDER_PATH=


# ── Optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything below has a working default. Uncomment a line only to override it.

# Absolute path to this project folder. The commands already find it when it is
# a connected folder, or sits in Desktop or OneDrive/Desktop; set this when it
# lives anywhere else, or when several copies exist.
#BONFIRE_HOME=

# Note: you must be using Blender 5.2 or newer for the Anthropic Claude MCP to work

# Where the MCP add-on's bridge server listens inside Blender. Change these
# only to match a change you made in Blender: Preferences → Add-ons → MCP.
#BLENDER_MCP_HOST=localhost
#BLENDER_MCP_PORT=9876


# ── Bambu ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Layer A (building print-ready 3MFs) needs none of these.

# Bambu Studio's executable, for slicing from the command line (Layer B).
# Found automatically in the usual places; set it only if it lives elsewhere.
#   Windows   C:\Program Files\Bambu Studio\bambu-studio.exe
#BAMBU_STUDIO_PATH=

# Bambu Cloud account, for reading and (later) controlling the P1S.
# Sign in once with:  python tools/bambu_cloud.py login <email>
# The password is typed there, hidden, and never stored anywhere — not here,
# not in any file. The resulting token (about three months) is kept in
# tools/bambu_cloud.local.json, which is git-ignored.
# The printer must be signed in to the same account, with LAN-only Mode and
# Developer Mode OFF — those two turn the cloud off.
#BAMBU_EMAIL=

# The printer's serial number, from the device list. Only needed if the
# account has more than one printer.
#BAMBU_PRINTER_SERIAL=

# The printer's home-network address and access code, for sending files and
# for the camera. Both are found automatically (the printer announces its
# address; the access code comes from Bambu Cloud), so set them only if that
# fails. Printer screen: Settings -> WLAN.
#BAMBU_PRINTER_IP=
#BAMBU_ACCESS_CODE=

# Print watch (tools/bambu_watch.py) — the failure detector. By default Obico's
# model runs right in Python (tools/bambu_detect.py) and needs nothing here.
# Only for the Docker version ("judge": "obico_docker" under camera_watch in
# tools/bambu_config.json, started with `python tools/bambu_watch.py obico up`):
#   OBICO_ML_URL    where it answers; default http://127.0.0.1:3333. Change the
#                   port here if 3333 is taken, then `obico up` again.
#   OBICO_ML_TOKEN  any long random string; the detector then only answers
#                   requests carrying it. Set it before `obico up` (and run
#                   `obico up` again whenever you change it).
#OBICO_ML_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3333
#OBICO_ML_TOKEN=

# Only if the print watch is switched to the Claude API instead of the local
# detector ("judge": "claude" under camera_watch in tools/bambu_config.json).
# Billed by the Claude API, separately from the Claude app. Never commit it.
#ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
